These entries are the Python charter's `docs/news/`, taken whole from
https://github.com/diazoxide/charter at commit 50d31dc. That is where they came from and nothing
more: no check ties this copy to that repository any more. The Python oracle that compared it
byte for byte is gone (ADR 0046) and the corpus is frozen history (ADR 0045), so it changes only
when somebody edits a file here on purpose.

WHY THERE ARE TWO COPIES AT ALL

`charter/news.py` says it plainly: a news entry travels with the code that implements it, and a
control plane has no reason to vendor a copy and every reason not to, because a copy drifts from
the binary invisibly and in both directions. The Python charter satisfies that with a wheel
(`docs/news` is force-included as `charter/_news`). A Rust charter shipping inside a signed app
has no checkout to fall back on, so the entries are compiled in — `build.rs` reads this
directory and `include_str!`s every file.

THIS IS FROZEN HISTORY (ADR 0045)

These are the Python charter's notes about its own releases, 0.44.0 to 0.62.1, and the few
entries it had staged when it stopped. None of them is news about this app, whose release notes
are `CHANGELOG.md`, and nothing is added here. They stay compiled in, read-only, so `charter
news` can still say what a plane pinned on the Python line skipped. `news::HISTORY_REPO` keeps
linking each entry into `diazoxide/charter` by tag, because that is where each one was published.

NO FILE IN HERE IS NOT AN ENTRY

`build.rs` compiles in every `*.md`, and `news::unreadable` reports any of them charter cannot
read as an entry — which the release gate then refuses on. So a note about this directory has to
be a file the glob does not match. This one is.
